stress: 10/11 ok; spike: 9/11 ok; HTTP request failures across all 22 runs: 0; probe failures (health/readiness/tenant/integrity, S5): 0

findings (honest):
F1 api-agents (GET /api/agents + /api/specializations): S3 stop at 10 s in stress (100/s) and spike (200/s). Root cause measured locally: /api/agents returns ALL 1000 agents (148,015 bytes) unpaginated, ~16 ms CPU per call (in-process median 16.3 ms, p90 21.3 ms) on the single event loop => ~60 calls/s ceiling for that route; stress sends ~50/s of it plus the other route. A capacity defect, not a crash: 0 failed requests. Fix options (pagination / snapshot cache / ETag) change the API contract => NOT changed in this batch.
F2 api-admin-tools (GET /api/admin/tools): stress 100/s flat (median ~6 ms for 300 s); spike 200/s => median per 10 s window 9.8, 9.6, 10.8, 11.1, 54.7, 250.4, 619.4, 983.9, 1173.9 ms: linear queue growth = offered load above the single worker's service rate (~5 ms CPU x 200/s ~ 1 core). S3 stopped at 85 s. Saturation, not a leak (stress was flat).
F3 api-tasks-submit spike: passed, but p99 122 ms and max 1669 ms vs p95 21.5 ms — tail sensitivity under 100/s submissions; within SLO.
F4 cross-cutting: production runs ONE uvicorn worker (render.yaml startCommand, Dockerfile CMD) and the state (TASKS, AGENT_REGISTRY, rate-limit, DAG runs) is in-process. CPU-bound routes therefore cap at ~1 core regardless of machine size: the Q-023 spec of a 16-vCPU SUT would NOT raise these ceilings without a multi-worker/shared-state design (RISK-036, Q-026).

what this does NOT prove:
- no capacity number: SUT, generator and storage share 2 physical cores (SMT); latencies include generator contention.
- volatile storage only (live-local storage stress/spike not run here).
- soak: separate log 390 (running at the time of this log).
- criteria in benchmarks/p6/acceptance_criteria.json stay 'proposed'; R-177 not done.
